# Project Rules — Nihongo Reader

You are working on a Flutter mobile app for Japanese reading and vocabulary learning.

## Product Summary

The app lets users scan or import Japanese text, extract it with OCR, read it in an interactive reader, look up words, save vocabulary, and review saved words as flashcards.

The MVP is a local-first mobile demo.

## Stack

Use:
- Flutter
- Dart
- Android and iOS only
- ML Kit for OCR
- SQLite for local persistence
- Local dictionary data
- Feature-based folder structure

Do not use unless explicitly requested:
- Firebase
- Supabase
- backend services
- authentication
- cloud sync
- payment systems
- subscriptions
- LLM APIs
- server-side infrastructure

## Architecture

Use feature-based folders:

- lib/features/ocr
- lib/features/reader
- lib/features/dictionary
- lib/features/flashcards
- lib/features/stats
- lib/core/db
- lib/core/routing
- lib/core/theme
- lib/core/widgets

Prefer simple, readable architecture.

Avoid over-engineering.

## Development Rules

- Make small, incremental changes.
- Do not implement the whole app in one step.
- Do not modify android/ or ios/ unless the task specifically requires native configuration.
- Keep platform-specific changes minimal and documented.
- Prefer local-first functionality.
- Keep UI simple and demo-friendly.
- Do not add unnecessary packages.
- Do not introduce backend dependencies.
- Do not add user accounts.
- Do not add monetization features.

## Quality Gates

After meaningful changes, run:

```bash
flutter analyze
flutter test